ID:DC The DOS Device Chain and DESQview
Quarterdeck Technical Note #286                    Filename: DEVCHAIN.TEC
by Brian P. Doud                                 CompuServe: DEVCHA.ZIP
Last revised: 9/09/94                              Category: DV

Subject: Explanation of the DOS device chain, and how to successfully
         run programs within DESQview that break the chain.


                               OVERVIEW
                               --------

In order to understand why stability of the device chain is critical in
DESQview, a brief explanation of how the chain works is necessary.

When a machine boots, DOS initializes itself.  At that point, all the
device drivers that are loaded are set up in a chain, which can be
referenced by all programs that load.  It is called a chain because each
driver has an embedded pointer which is used to refer to the address of
the next driver in sequence, thus chaining them together.  As additional
drivers are added, they are placed at the top, or beginning, of the
chain.

This works fine in DOS, which is single-tasking.  However, in DESQview,
which is multitasking, the integrity of the device chain can be
compromised.

With earlier versions of DESQview, the device chain is not saved and
restored for each DESQview window; rather, each window handles the
device chain separate from the others. If a program puts itself in the
device chain when it starts, it is applicable only to the window in
which it is loaded, though the driver has linked itself to the DOS
device chain globally.

For example, a program such as WordPerfect refers to device drivers in
the chain during normal operation.  If a program in another window has
put itself in the device chain, WordPerfect would expect the addresses
of all the drivers to apply to its window as well.  This would not be
the case, and WordPerfect would crash upon reading the invalid address,
that of the new program in the chain.

There are a few examples of programs that place themselves in the device
chain, thereby breaking the chain and requiring that it be restored:
the Peachtree accounting system; the Banner network broadcast program,
included with the Banyan Vines network; and IRMA 3270 emulation
software.  Once it is determined that the device chain has indeed been
broken, restoring it is a relatively simple process.

                         DESQview 2.40-2.43
                         ------------------

With DESQview 2.40-2.43, a means of maintaining the DOS device chain for
all windows was made possible.  This is through the DEVICE.COM program,
which was updated to use XDI (External Device Interface) calls to save
and restore the device chain.  When DEVICE.COM is used to load a small
device driver (such as ANSI.SYS) prior to the actual program loading,
the program can be kept from appearing in the device chains of the other
windows.




Rather than loading the program on its own, it is necessary to load a
device driver in the window first.  This can be done by creating a short
batch file.  For example, one possible batch file to load Peachtree
(PT.BAT for this example) would read:

     cd\dv
     device c:\dos\ansi.sys
     cd\[name of Peachtree directory]
     pt.exe

The name of the batch file would then be entered on the PROGRAM NAME
line on the Standard Options screen in the Change A Program for
Peachtree.  When this is done, the device chain will be kept intact, and
subsequent windows will load without crashing.

                     DESQview 2.60 and DESQview/X
                     ----------------------------

Only a small number of programs put themselves in the DOS device chain
and cause these problems.  As of DESQview 2.60 and in all versions of
DESQview/X, the DOS device chain is saved and restored internally,
allowing programs which do this to function properly without the
assistance of an additional driver being loaded in the DESQview window.
Because of this, the alternate solution to the problem is to upgrade to
the latest version of DESQview.  This can be done by contacting our
Customer Service department at (800)354-3222.

